            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to perish, then the Latin for a guard."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: pariō, parere",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pariō, parere — \"to give birth to; to produce\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Uxor eius fīliam peperit. His wife gave birth to a daughter."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pariō, parere, peperī, partum, short a. Not pāreō, pārēre, 'to obey' (taught). English parent comes from its family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pariō is inherited from a Proto-Indo-European root meaning \"to bring forth\" (EtymDB). English parent comes from its participle parēns."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
